# Regression discontinuity: what happens at your 65th birthday
next: {"title":"Stress-testing a discontinuity","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/stress-testing-a-discontinuity.cast"}

## A Birthday as an Experiment
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[55,75],"y":[0,100]}

    axes ax x_label "Age (years)" y_label "Percent of people" hidden true
    curve cov_left #2f6b8f expr "83 + 0.5*(x-55)" x_from 55 x_to 65 hidden true
    curve cov_right #2f6b8f expr "98.5 + 0.03*(x-65)" x_from 65 x_to 75 hidden true
    label label_cov "E[D_i | age]: any coverage" below #2f6b8f attach_to cov_right hidden true

Over these five parts we'll ask whether one rule — turn sixty-five, and Medicare switches on — can do the work of an experiment, and what it revealed about whether coverage keeps very sick people alive.
    draw ax

Here is the share of Americans holding any health insurance, drifting gently upward through the late fifties and early sixties — the probability of coverage at each age, E of D_i given age.
    draw cov_left label_cov

Just short of sixty-five it sits near eighty-eight percent: roughly one in eight of these adults has no coverage at all.
    highlight cov_left effect glow

    pause 0.3

Now mark the sixty-fifth birthday, the day Medicare eligibility begins for nearly every American who paid into Social Security.
    edge cutoff dashed #8f887c from.x 65 from.y 0 to.x 65 to.y 100
    label label_cutoff "65th birthday" above #8f887c attach_to cutoff

One birthday later, the same measure is pressed up against one hundred percent.
    draw cov_right delivery grave

    pause 0.5

About ten points of coverage appear overnight — a genuine step in a variable that otherwise only drifts.
    arrow jump_arrow #b5482e from.x 65.6 from.y 88.1 to.x 65.6 to.y 98.4
    label label_jump "≈ +10 points overnight" right #b5482e attach_to jump_arrow

    pause 0.4

The obvious objection is that sixty-six-year-olds differ from sixty-four-year-olds in a hundred ways, so watch what else moves: smoking falls steadily with age.
    curve smoke #8a5fa8 expr "20 - 0.45*(x-55)"
    label label_smoke "Currently smokes" below #8a5fa8 attach_to smoke

Chronic illness climbs steadily — and both cross the birthday without so much as a kink.
    curve chronic #8a5fa8 expr "44 + 1.1*(x-55)"
    label label_chronic "Has a chronic condition" above-left #8a5fa8 attach_to chronic

Income, schooling and marital status behave the same way, because nothing inside a person changes on a birthday; only a legal rule does.
    focus smoke chronic cutoff

And that rule is an accident of politics — Lyndon Johnson signed Medicare in July 1965 and handed card number one to Harry Truman, who had pushed for national health insurance twenty years earlier.
    portrait truman of "Harry S. Truman" cameo true

The compromise settled on the number sixty-five, and an arbitrary number is exactly what a research design can exploit.
    erase truman

So strip the picture back to the step itself.
    clear keep ["ax","cov_left","cov_right","label_cov","cutoff","label_cutoff","jump_arrow","label_jump"]

And come in close on the weeks either side of the line.
    camera center.ref jump_arrow zoom 1.8

Compare someone a month short of sixty-five with someone a month past: same smoking, same income, same diseases — different insurance.
    dot p_before #b5482e at.x 64.7 at.y 87.9
    dot p_after #b5482e at.x 65.3 at.y 98.5
    label label_pair "64y 11m vs. 65y 1m" below-left #b5482e attach_to p_before

That step is the design — assignment to coverage decided by the calendar rather than by anything about the patient.
    mark mark target label_jump kind box

In Oregon a lottery drew about thirty thousand names from ninety thousand applicants; here a birthday does the drawing, for everyone, every day.
    camera reset true

A rule, not a lottery, can manufacture quasi-random variation — and whether the comparison across it is legitimate rests on a single assumption, which is where we go next.

## Continuity, Not Parallel Trends
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}

    axes ax x_label "Age (running variable)" y_label "E[Y_i | age]" hidden true
    arrow cut_line dashed #8f887c from.x 50 from.y 0 to.x 50 to.y 88 hidden true
    label label_cut "Age 65" above #8f887c attach_to cut_line hidden true
    curve y0_left #2f6b8f expr 30+0.35*x x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    curve y0_right dashed #2f6b8f expr 30+0.35*x x_from 50 x_to 100 hidden true
    curve y1_right #b5482e expr 46+0.35*x x_from 50 x_to 100 hidden true
    curve y1_left dashed #b5482e expr 46+0.35*x x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    label label_y0 "E[Y_i(0) | age]" below #2f6b8f attach_to y0_left hidden true
    label label_y1 "E[Y_i(1) | age]" above #b5482e attach_to y1_right hidden true
    arrow jump_arrow #8a5fa8 from.x 50 from.y 47.5 to.x 50 to.y 63.5 hidden true
    label label_tau "τ: the jump at the cutoff" right #8a5fa8 attach_to jump_arrow hidden true
    curve y0_local #8a5fa8 expr 30+0.35*x x_from 41 x_to 50 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    curve y1_local #8a5fa8 expr 46+0.35*x x_from 50 x_to 59 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label label_local "Identified here, and only here" below #8a5fa8 attach_to y0_local hidden true
    mark mark_local target label_local kind box hidden true

Now that we've seen coverage jump at the 65th birthday, let's state exactly what has to be true for that jump to be causal.
    draw ax cut_line label_cut

Left of the line we observe the untreated conditional mean — hospital use for people without Medicare, drifting up as they age.
    draw y0_left label_y0

Right of the line almost everyone has D_i equal to one, so what we see there is the treated conditional mean.
    draw y1_right label_y1

    pause 0.4

The dashed halves are what we never observe: each side's missing counterfactual.
    draw y0_right y1_left parallel true

The RD estimate is this vertical gap — the distance between the two curves at exactly age 65.
    draw jump_arrow label_tau

And it is causal only under continuity: both conditional expectations cross the birthday without a break.
    highlight y0_right y1_left effect glow

In plain words — everything else that shapes health care use runs smoothly through the day you turn sixty-five; only D_i breaks.
    point at.ref cut_line gesture tap

Card, Dobkin and Maestas found exactly that pattern: coverage jumps by roughly ten percentage points at 65, while income, education, race and marital status trend straight through the birthday.

The design is older than it looks — Thistlethwaite and Campbell invented it in 1960, comparing students just above and just below a National Merit test-score cutoff.
    delivery soft

Now hold that against the assumption we made back in lecture eleven.
    clear

Difference-in-differences: two groups moving together, until a policy lands on one of them.
    axes ax2 x_label Year y_label "Outcome Y_i"
    arrow policy_line dashed #8f887c from.x 50 from.y 0 to.x 50 to.y 88
    label label_policy "Policy" above #8f887c attach_to policy_line
    curve control_line #2f6b8f expr 35+0.3*x x_from 0 x_to 100
    label label_control "Control" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to control_line
    curve treated_pre #b5482e expr 50+0.3*x x_from 0 x_to 50
    curve treated_post #b5482e expr 65+0.55*(x-50) x_from 50 x_to 100
    label label_treated "Treated" above #b5482e attach_to treated_post

Parallel trends asks this dashed path to stay right for every year afterwards — a claim about a whole horizon nobody ever observes.
    curve counterfactual dashed #d0865f expr 50+0.3*x x_from 50 x_to 100
    label label_cf "Assumed counterfactual" below-right #d0865f attach_to counterfactual

Continuity asks much less: not that two groups track each other for a decade, but that nothing else breaks in a small neighbourhood of one point.
    highlight counterfactual effect glow

That weaker assumption, though, is bought at a real price.
    clear

    show ax cut_line label_cut y0_left y0_right y1_left y1_right label_y0 label_y1 jump_arrow label_tau

Zoom in, and the estimand is a treatment effect at a single point: E[Y_i(1) − Y_i(0) given age equals 65].
    camera center.ref jump_arrow zoom 1.9

Only the people crowded against the line do the identifying work — nothing here speaks about a 55-year-old, or an 80-year-old.
    draw y0_local y1_local label_local mark_local

The Oregon lottery, by contrast, randomised among low-income adults from 19 to 64: its effect belongs to a whole population of compliers, not to one birthday.
    camera reset true

So parallel trends buys breadth with a strong assumption stretched across time; continuity buys credibility with a weak assumption at a point — and you can only ever spend it at that point.
    delivery grave

## Estimating the Jump
voice: female
level: advanced
use: rd_plot
with:
    jump: 2.2
    slope: 0.15
    curvature: 0.008
    noise: 0.3
    x_label: Age minus 65 (years)
    cutoff_label: 65th birthday
    title: Share with any health insurance

Now that we have seen that RD rests on continuity rather than parallel trends, let's turn a birthday into an actual number.
    draw axes title

First, recenter the running variable: age minus 65, so the cutoff sits at zero and D_i switches on the instant it is crossed.
    draw cutoff_line

Each dot is a bin of ages — say one month of age — and its average coverage; just before the birthday, roughly 87 percent of Americans hold any health insurance.
    draw dots_left

Just after, it is close to 98 percent, because Medicare eligibility is essentially universal at 65.
    draw dots_right

    pause 0.4

Now fit a line to the left-hand bins — but only to those inside a bandwidth h, say two years, since 50-year-olds tell us almost nothing about people at the cutoff.
    draw fit_left

Then a separate line on the right: the regression is Y_i equals alpha, plus tau times D_i, plus beta times age-minus-65, plus gamma times the interaction.
    draw fit_right

That interaction term is what lets each side keep its own slope — force a single common slope and any curvature in the age profile leaks straight into your estimate.
    highlight fit_left fit_right effect glow

Extrapolate both lines to the boundary and read the vertical gap between them: tau, about ten percentage points of coverage.
    draw jump_bracket

That circled gap is the whole estimate — not a comparison of 60-year-olds with 70-year-olds, but two extrapolations meeting at one point.
    mark mark_jump target jump_bracket kind circle
    delivery grave

And since D here is coverage itself, this jump is the first stage — the health outcomes will later be divided by it.
    point at.ref cutoff_line gesture tap

Now the choice that does most of the work: shrink h, and only near-65s count — little bias, but few observations, so the estimate wobbles.
    camera center.ref cutoff_line zoom 2

Widen h and you borrow strength from 62- and 68-year-olds — more precision, but now a straight line must summarize a curved age profile, and that curvature becomes bias.
    camera reset true

Because that trade-off is unavoidable, applied work reports a data-driven bandwidth — the Imbens–Kalyanaraman rule, or Calonico, Cattaneo and Titiunik's.
    highlight dots_left dots_right effect pulse

The design is older than the health economics: Thistlethwaite and Campbell invented it in 1960, comparing students just above and just below a National Merit award cutoff.
    highlight cutoff_line effect glow

So an RD estimate is a difference between two fitted lines at a single point on the age axis — and its credibility lives entirely inside the window you chose.

## What the Cutoff Can and Cannot Tell You
voice: female
level: advanced
use: rd_plot
with:
    jump: -1
    slope: 0.18
    curvature: 0.006
    noise: 0.3
    x_label: Age at admission
    cutoff_label: 65th birthday
    title: 7-day mortality, non-deferrable ED admissions

    path age_axis #8f887c points [[120,430],[900,430]] hidden true
    text tick19 "19" #8f887c x 160 y 398 font_size 20 hidden true
    text tick64 "64" #8f887c x 610 y 398 font_size 20 hidden true
    text tick85 "85" #8f887c x 830 y 398 font_size 20 hidden true
    path oregon_band #2f6b8f points [[160,430],[616,430]] style.opacity 0.5 style.stroke_width 16 hidden true
    text oregon_label "Oregon lottery: uninsured adults 19–64" #2f6b8f x 370 y 478 font_size 22 hidden true
    shape rd_dot #b5482e shape circle x 626 y 430 radius 9 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text rd_label "Medicare RD: age 65" #b5482e x 775 y 478 font_size 22 hidden true
    arrow rd_arrow rd_label -> rd_dot #b5482e hidden true
    text b1 "Lottery → LATE for compliers" #2f6b8f x 290 y 330 font_size 24 hidden true
    text b2 "A population, not a moment" x 290 y 292 font_size 21 hidden true
    text b3 "30,000 drawn, ~1 in 4 enrolled" #8f887c x 290 y 256 font_size 19 hidden true
    text r1 "Continuity → the effect at 65" #b5482e x 730 y 330 font_size 24 hidden true
    text r2 "A moment, not a population" x 730 y 292 font_size 21 hidden true
    text r3 "Admissions +10%, 7-day mortality −1 pt" #8f887c x 730 y 256 font_size 19 hidden true

Now that we have seen how the fuzzy jump in coverage at 65 gets scaled into a treatment effect, the question that decides whether you may cite it in a policy memo is left: whose effect is it?
    draw axes

Each dot is the seven-day death rate for patients wheeled into an emergency department with something that could not wait — a heart attack, a hip fracture — grouped by their age at admission.
    draw title dots_left dots_right

Fit the age trend separately on each side of the sixty-fifth birthday; nothing about these patients bends as they age through their early sixties.
    draw fit_left fit_right cutoff_line

    pause 0.4

Card, Dobkin and Maestas found the death rate falls by about one percentage point exactly at the line — roughly a fifth of a baseline near five percent.
    draw jump_bracket

The same birthday raises hospital admissions by roughly ten percent: more care bought, and for the sickest patients, fewer deaths.
    point at.ref dots_right gesture tap

What actually jumps at 65 is Medicare eligibility — the uninsured share among the near-elderly, around a tenth at 64, nearly vanishes overnight — while income, education, and a lifetime of smoking drift smoothly straight through the birthday.
    point at.ref cutoff_line gesture underline

So the identifying assumption is just this: potential outcomes are continuous at the cutoff — what mortality would have been without Medicare does not jump on a birthday.
    highlight fit_left fit_right effect glow

That is a gentler demand than parallel trends: we never claim two groups would have moved together for years, only that one curve would have stayed smooth for an instant — and in exchange we learn about an instant.
    focus cutoff_line jump_bracket

    pause 0.5

    clear

Put both designs of this course on one age axis.
    draw age_axis tick19 tick64 tick85

The Oregon lottery covered low-income uninsured adults from nineteen to sixty-four — about thirty thousand names drawn out of ninety thousand who signed up, and only about one in four of those drawn ended up enrolled.
    draw oregon_band oregon_label b3

The Medicare discontinuity lives at a single point on that axis: sixty-five.
    draw rd_dot rd_label rd_arrow r3

Randomization bought a local average effect for the lottery's compliers — a whole population of poor adults, though only in one state, and only those the lottery actually moved.
    draw b1 b2

Continuity bought something sharper and thinner: the effect at the cutoff, for people within months of that birthday, many already sick enough to be at an emergency-room door.
    draw r1 r2

Drag the RD estimate away from 65 and it stops being identified; drag Oregon's estimate up to the elderly and it stops being relevant.
    focus b2 r2

Both are local: Oregon's compliers are a kind of person, Medicare's are a person at a moment — and neither hands you the average effect of insurance on Americans.
    text closing "Both are local — neither is the average effect of insurance." x 500 y 175 font_size 26
    mark closing_box target closing kind box
    delivery grave

Across this series a birthday gave us an experiment nobody ran; what it can never give us is the world on either side of the cutoff.

